] [17592186164976 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fba6a292-c57f-5e96-94ce-9e2c7ba89798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164977 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2π}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164977 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164977 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "229f67ff-466c-50df-836f-deeb895b8748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166562 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166562 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x) = \\cos(2x)$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5888d4ce-bc45-540b-afa0-901008abfcde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166562 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in[0,\\pi).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi)=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi$-periodic, because for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=f(0)=F(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\neq f(x)$ for $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166563 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166563 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) g(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ defined above, compute $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(6x), \\cos(4x) \\rangle$ and determine if functions $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "052f11a0-2f27-5285-83af-6d8fdb729024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166563 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\sin(6x)\\cos(4x)\n=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\sin(10x)+\\sin(2x)\\big).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166564 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166564 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right).\n$$\n\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2127a8e-14bb-5574-a542-852333fe4cca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166564 #edb/ref 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left( a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0,$ $a_n,$ and $b_n$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={a}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1039 "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1