{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e763dfc-e659-54e6-9b52-205e19a11f13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314970 #edb/ref 1074 "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314989 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314989 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314989 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{1}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314989 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c36e0e36-a227-5df1-b273-538c9f56de6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314989 #edb/ref 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩ & ={b}_{1}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{1} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
